ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova
==================================================
Politician

journalist

Iliana Iotova [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-2]  ( [LINK: (née) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birth_name#Maiden_and_married_names]  Malinova; born 24 October 1964) is a Bulgarian politician and journalist who has served as the  [LINK: (president of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Bulgaria]  since 23 January 2026. She is the  [LINK: (first woman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_elected_and_appointed_female_state_leaders]  to hold the position. A member of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party]  (BSP), Iotova previously served as the  [LINK: (vice president) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_President_of_Bulgaria]  under  [LINK: (Rumen Radev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev]  from 2017 to 2026.


# EARLY LIFE #

Iliana Malinova Iotova was born in  [LINK: (Sofia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofia] ,  [LINK: (People's Republic of Bulgaria) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Republic_of_Bulgaria]  on 24 October 1964. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto2-3]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  She studied in the  [LINK: (Lycée Français de Sofia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyc%C3%A9e_Fran%C3%A7ais_de_Sofia] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto4-5]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  She received a degree in Bulgarian and French  [LINK: (philology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philology]  from the  [LINK: (University of Sofia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Sofia]  and later specialized at the  [LINK: (École nationale d'administration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cole_nationale_d%27administration]  (ENA, National School of Administration) in  [LINK: (Strasbourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strasbourg] , France, and the Center for European Studies (CEES) of the  [LINK: (University of Strasbourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Strasbourg] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]

In the 1980s, Malinova worked as a nurse at the First Hospital of Sofia St John the Baptist. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-vesti-6]  From 1990 to 1997, she worked at  [LINK: (Bulgarian National Television) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_National_Television]  as a reporter, editor, director, presenter of news and current affairs programmes and also became head of the News and Current Affairs Directorate. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto4-5]  [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bnr-7]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  She then worked as the director of the press service of the  [LINK: (Bulgarian Socialist Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_Socialist_Party]  in 1997. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-8]  [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bnr-7]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]


# POLITICAL CAREER #

In the  [LINK: (2005 national elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2005_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election] , Iotova was elected to the  [LINK: (National Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Assembly_(Bulgaria)] , an office she held until 2007. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bnr-7]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  During her term, she headed the Bulgarian delegation to the  [LINK: (Assemblée parlementaire de la Francophonie) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembl%C3%A9e_parlementaire_de_la_Francophonie]  and member of the delegation to the  [LINK: (Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliamentary_Assembly_of_the_Council_of_Europe] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]

Iotova became a  [LINK: (Member of the European Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_of_the_European_Parliament]  in 2007 and was re-elected in 2014. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto1-9]  There she was part of the  [LINK: (Progressive Alliance of Socialists and Democrats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_Alliance_of_Socialists_and_Democrats]  group. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto1-9]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]

In parliament, Iotova served on the  [LINK: (Committee on the Internal Market and Consumer Protection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Committee_on_the_Internal_Market_and_Consumer_Protection]  (2007–09), the  [LINK: (Committee on Fisheries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Committee_on_Fisheries]  (2009–14), and the  [LINK: (Committee on Petitions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Committee_on_Petitions]  (2009–14). [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto2-3]  From 2012 until 2013, she was also a member of the Special Committee on Organised Crime, Corruption and Money Laundering. From the  [LINK: (2014 elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_European_Parliament_election] , she served as vice-chairwoman of the  [LINK: (Committee on Civil Liberties, Justice and Home Affairs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Committee_on_Civil_Liberties,_Justice_and_Home_Affairs] , under the leadership of chairman  [LINK: (Claude Moraes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Moraes] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto4-5]  [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto2-3]  In this capacity, she was her parliamentary group's  [LINK: (rapporteur) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapporteur]  on a 2015 report calling for the equitable distribution of 40,000 refugees across the European Union. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-10]

In addition to her committee assignments, Iotova served as chairwoman of the parliament's delegation to the EU-Montenegro Stabilisation and Association Parliamentary Committee and as member of the delegation to the  [LINK: (Parliamentary Assembly of the Mediterranean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliamentary_Assembly_of_the_Mediterranean] . [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-auto3-11]  She was also a member of the European Parliament Intergroup on Integrity (Transparency, Anti-Corruption and Organized Crime). [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-12]

Ahead of Bulgaria's  [LINK: (2016 presidential elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Bulgarian_presidential_election] , Iotova was officially nominated as the Bulgarian Socialist Party's running mate for presidential candidate  [LINK: (Rumen Radev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev] . After her election to the Vice Presidency, she resigned her MEP seat. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-13]  As Vice President, Iotova focused on relations with Bulgarian communities abroad and on promoting Bulgarian education and language. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bnr-7]  They both were sworn in on 19 January 2017 and began their office on 22 January. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-14]  [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-Officially-15]

On 1 February 2021, President Radev officially announced that he and Iliana Iotova would run for a second term in the  [LINK: (2021 general election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Bulgarian_general_election] . [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-16]  In the second round, held on 21 November, Rumen Radev defeated Gerdjikov with 66% of the vote, thus securing a second term in office for Iotova as well. [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-17]  She was sworn in on 19 January 2022 and her term officially began on 22 January. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]

On 21 March 2024, Iotova participated in an educational forum in Lisbon, which she sponsored, and in which she called for the need to create a Bulgarian cultural institution abroad to promote Bulgarian culture and language internationally. [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-18]

During an address to the nation on 19 January 2026, the then-president  [LINK: (Rumen Radev) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumen_Radev]  announced his intention to resign. [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-19]  The following day, Radev submitted his resignation as President to the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Bulgaria] . [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-20]  Three days later on 23 January, the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Bulgaria]  formally accepted Radev's resignation, thus ending his tenure as President of Bulgaria. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-21]  Shortly after the ruling, Radev left the "Dondukov 2" palace together with Iotova, the new President, and addressed supporters, promising a unified struggle at the upcoming legislative elections. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-22]  Iliana Iotova officially became Bulgaria's first female president on 23 January 2026, ahead of the planned  [LINK: (snap election) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Bulgarian_parliamentary_election]  to complete Radev's presidential term. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-Officially-15]


# PERSONAL LIFE #

Aside from her native  [LINK: (Bulgarian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_language] , she is fluent in  [LINK: (French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_language]  and speaks  [LINK: (Russian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_language]  and  [LINK: (English) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]  Iotova married Andrey Iotov, a doctor she met at the First Hospital of Sofia St. John the Baptist, in 1985, and they have one child. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-vesti-6]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-bta1-4]


# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_ref-2]   [LINK: (Bulgarian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulgarian_language] : Илияна Малинова Йотова, pronounced  [LINK: ([iliˈjanɐ mɐ'linovɐ 'jotovɐ]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Bulgarian] . Sometimes spelled as Iliyana Malinova Yotova [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliana_Iotova#cite_note-1]  as per the country's  [LINK: (official romanization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Bulgarian] .


